  "notes": [
    "the discovery intervals cover 7016/7104 unique query bases (98.8%); dosage is the mean multiplicity over covered bases, and the bracket [2.258, 2.308] assigns the 88 uncovered bases zero copies and the largest multiplicity observed on the query respectively.",
    "query-coordinate spans overlap by 5915 bp (83.3% of the gene); under the collapse model a query base resolved into several fragments carries the sum of their ratios, so overlap raises the per-base multiplicity (max 4.016) rather than counting bases twice. The same geometry with every region at single copy would return 1.843, so the dosage is NOT a copy count here; that figure is itself a lower bound on the inflation, because multiply represented sequence also attracts multi-mapped reads that raise the per-region ratios.",
    "2 of 9 locus regions have zero filtered depth, so the query bases they resolve contribute nothing to the dosage. The estimate is biased downward by that amount.",
    "the interval [1.111, 1.349] excludes 1 from above, which does not on its own indicate a departure from single copy: it covers within-locus sampling noise only, and single-copy loci commonly produce intervals that exclude 1. The verdict, not the interval, carries the copy-number statement.",
    "the interval contains no integer; it is narrower than the spacing between adjacent copy numbers and cannot be read as an interval for an integer copy count."
  ],
